From: hedrick@farside.rutgers.edu (Charles Hedrick)
Subject: (more or less) final conclusion on Ethernet problem with TULIP
Date: 1995/05/31
Message-ID: <3qj497$8f7@farside.rutgers.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 103622261
organization: Rutgers University LCSR
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

A week or so ago I reported a problem with a new Alpha workstation
that has the TULIP Ethernet interface.  It was dropping a lot of
packets, in a location where a Sun works fine.  We've now got a more
or less final resolution of the problem.  After finding that the
workstation worked fine in another office, we checked the cable to the
initial office.  The TDR claimed it was 385 feet, and rejected it.
This is over the maximum cable length allowed by the 10baseT spec.  We
reran the wire, and managed to get it down to 360 feet.  The new run
used level 5 wire, while the old one used level 3.  The cable tester
now says the run is within spec, and the Alpha works fine.  I'm still
a bit concerned that the Alpha is more sensitive to the cable length
and/or attenuation than other systems.  But I guess I can't complain
when it fails on a run that the cable tester rejects.  Now, anybody
know how we go about returning a building for a refund?  (This isn't
the only problem: the doors don't close; the floors don't stay down;
pipes burst every winter because they're not properly insulated.
Don't ever move into a low-bid building.)



