Our network environment in my research group includes several DEC
Alpha workstations and several Sun workstations. We formerly used a
Sun NIS server for our whole subnet, and we configured the NIS
database Makefile on the NIS server to pass the -b flag to makedbm so
that the server would generate DNS requests for names not resolvable
in the NIS hosts maps.

We now need to use an OSF/1 3.0 Alpha machine as a slave NIS server
for a subnet.

The /etc/svc.conf file in OSF/1 3.0 appears to allow _client_ machines
to search via DNS if NIS fails to return a match for a hostname, but
it is my understanding that ypserv does _not_ pay attention to
/etc/svc.conf, but only looks in the NIS maps and returns a match
failure when no match is found.

Because our Sun machines aren't easily configurable to generate DNS
requests themselves when NIS lookups fail (the shared libraries need
to all be changed to use DNS _instead_ of NIS, which isn't what we
want the Sun clients to do), we really need our OSF/1 3.0 ypserv
process to mimic the Sun behavior of generating DNS requests on behalf
of its NIS clients when necessary.

makedbm in OSF/1 3.0 does not appear to take the -b flag to mark a map
as allowing DNS request generation the way SunOS makedbm
does. Furthermore, when we ypxfr the hosts maps from the master Sun
machine, the Alpha either strips the -b flag in the dbm files or
leaves it in, but ypserv ignores it.

It seems like there _must_ be a way to force an OSF/1 ypserv process
to generate DNS requests when NIS hosts map lookups fail.

Does anyone know how to do this?
