Date: 1995/05/30
Message-ID: <3qfcva$q42@homer.alpha.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 103490848
organization: McHugh Freeman
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.programmer

I have the requirement to be able to determine the physical location
(within a facility) of a user that has telnetted in to a Unix,
specifically DEC OSF/1, host from a terminal server.

Basically we need to know what port their using on the terminal server.
(Note that this is not simply the "random" pty that the tty command or
ttyname function report.)

Another reason you might want such a facility is to access a serial
device that is connected to a terminal server, as if it where physically
connected to a serial port on the machine.

I've seen these referred to as an "ocd" (Outbound Connection Daemon) or
"rtelnet" (for Reverse TELNET.)

Is there anything that's somewhat to portable that provides this kind of
functionality, any 3rd part vendor products?

DEC OSF/1 solution: ???
------------------
Is LAT a possibility here?

HP-UX solution:
--------------
Using HP's DDFA software, and a terminal server, you start and "ocd"
(Outbound Connection Daemon) that negotiates the connection from the
Unix host to a specified port on a terminal server.  We have done this
with HP's own DTC terminal servers and Xyplex terminal servers.

 DESCRIPTION
      The Outbound Connection Daemon (ocd) is part of the HP Data
      Communications and Terminal Controller (DTC) Device File Access (DDFA)
      software.  It manages the connection and data transfer to the remote
      DTC port.

Solaris solution:
----------------
Using Sun's rtelnet daemon for their Annex terminal servers, you can,
likewise, associate a pty with a given physical port on the terminal
server.

DESCRIPTION
     Rtelnet is  a  modified  version  of  the  Berkeley  telnetd
     program.   It  will  create a pseudo-tty (pty) device on the
     host that is connected via TELNET or raw  TCP  to  an  Annex
     serial  port.  This allows UNIX programs such as tip(1C) and
     uucp(1C) to access serial devices attached to  Annex  ports.
     Rtelnet  works like a telnet daemon program (telnetd) except
     in a reverse fashion; instead of accepting connections  from
     other   network   machines,  rtelnet  makes  connections  to
     Annexes.  Rtelnet creates a new file-like device (usually in
     /dev)  that programs can open and use like a normal /dev/tty
     terminal.

Please email any info.
Thanks!
Dave

