We've just installed two of the new PCI-based Alpha desktop systems,
the 250 4/266.  We've having very high rates of packet losses.  The
simplest way to reproduce is to ping another machine on the network
with a packetsize of 8000.  (This is intended to simulate NFS traffic,
which uses a packet size of slightly over 8000.)  There's a loss rate
of over 50%.  This is occuring with two different machines, but
they're being tested in the same office.  We've plugged them into a
10baseT connection where a Sun is working fine, and we've tried two
different 10baseT hubs in the machine room, but we haven't tried
changing the building wiring (or room), though we've tried two
different outlets in the room and two different cables (one of which
is from DEC).  The network involved has lots of Suns, and probably
fairly high traffic levels.  The last time we were involved with DEC
was in the days of DEUNA's.  They had trouble with the back to back
packets that Sun generated.  (At one point some people had accused Sun
of violating the spec's, but that was quickly shown to be false.)  We
had assumed that by now this problem was fixed.  Are we overly
optimistic?  

We don't see this problem with 3000/300LX's.  The same kind of
interface is on a 2100 we have.  It doesn't show the same high packet
loss rates when tested with ping, but a number of users have noted NFS
performance problems, and I've seen problems with delays in response
in xterm.  (I'm running on a 3000/300LX.)

We've had field service in for two successive days looking at the
machines for other reasons.  They've been unsuccessful in getting
their own support people in Colorado to call them back for two days.
So I'm hoping someone here may know something useful.



