In article <3pvkel$8n7@post.gsfc.nasa.gov> jmasino@gtpitch.gsfc.nasa.gov (Jay Masino) writes:


Jay> Does anyone know of a public domain ramdisk program that'll run
Jay> under OSF/1 V3.0 ??  We want to test a proman memory-to-memory
Jay> that presently uses a lot of disk I/O with getc and putc.

You don't need any extra software, Digital Unix already has a memory
filesystem builtin to the system.  All you need to do is read the
'mfs' man page.  As a quick example you could do this:

	mkdir /ram
	mfs /dev/rz#b /ram

where /dev/rz#b is your primary swap partition.  See the man page for
details.

