Hello  Adrian,
I can tell u what i do when I get the unaligned access messages. 
When u declare the variables in the main(), take care in declaring the
variables of the largest size first, and then declare the smaller ones
laters.
It might help to add a dummy variable like this

main()
double dummy ; /* force declarations of subsequent vars along the word
boundary */
then delclare the structures, the character buffers and then pointers
provided the structures are greater than 8 bytes and so are the character
buffers, and finally declare the integers and last the characters (they are
1 byte) .;

I have observed that when I do this,the mesg disappears. Also u will be
surprised in the manner in which the structure members are declared. Declare
the larger ones first and then the smaller ones
for example
struct XXX_fo {
		long x ;  /* 8 bytes */
		char *str ; /* 8 bytes */
		int	 some_no ; /*4 bytes */
		char c ; /* 1 byte */
	} ;

Do try rearranging the order in which the classes/structures are defineed
and I do feel there would be a drastic improvement. I work on a DEC Alpha
machine too and have observed the same problems
sirish

the message is generated because the alpha 64 accesses data structures along
64 bit word boudaries and is very particular about that . 

