>>>>> "PAUL" == PAUL D CHAPIN <pdchapin@news.amherst.edu> writes:
In article <3q00kc$ora@amhux3.amherst.edu> pdchapin@news.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) writes:

PAUL> Am I correct in assuming that I can't nfs mount an advfs file
PAUL> system?  If it can be done, why is it giving me a "Can't access
PAUL> /usr: Permission denied" message?

No, you can export Advfs filesystems via NFS to other machines.  You
most likely problem is that you haven't setup your system to export
directories.  Check if you have nfsd running on your system, if yes,
then you should edit the /etc/exports file to look something like
this:

	/usr -r=0 host1,host2,host3, ... ,hostN

Though here we use netgroups instead of lists of hosts because we
server disks to 130+ machines.

If you don't have nfsd running on your system, the simplest thing to
do is run 'nfssetup' and answer the questions it asks.

