pch@mystech.com (Pete Halverson) wrote:
>We're doing some performance testing runs on our AXP3000's as part of a
>comparitive purchase evaluation, and one of the things I'd like to do is
>see what happens when running with *less* physical memory than that which
>we currently have installed (e.g. 64Mb vs. 96Mb).   Rather than physically
>pulling SIMMs, it occured to me that maybe I could emulate a
>reduced-memory configuration by forcibly wiring down 32Mb or so of pmem,
>preventing those blocks from being used or paged out.
>
>Does this sound plausible?  If so, does anyone have any
>suggestions/information about how to actually go about this?  Any other
>ideas about how to make a 96Mb box behave like it's really a 64Mb box?
>
>thanks,
>pch

The best way to simulate less physical memory is as follows:

1.  Boot the machine interactively
	>>> boot -fl i
2.  When prompted for image name, answer as follows to simulate 48Mb:
	vmunix memlimit=48

To undo this, or to increase the memory a little closer to your actual
memory size, you MUST power-cycle your machine.  Rebooting will not do
the trick.

I have used this several times, and avoided the inconvenience of pulling
SIMMs.

Ram




