In article <3phpip$ek@www.it.net>, Pier Luigi RATTO <pier@gepf44.atr.ansaldo.it> writes:

>I tried to create a library composed of 81 object files (8600 kbytes) with
>the following command:
>
>ar rv lib.a *.o
>
>I got the following error message:
>
>/: write failed, file system is full
>
>but the file system is only 15% occupied.

Which file system?  Unless you've made root very much larger than usual
I don't know of ANY root file system that is only 15% full.  And the
message *is* telling you which file system filled up.  As mentioned by 
the another poster ar(1) writes temporary files in /tmp.

It is also important to realize that ar(1) almost certainly cleans up
after itself when it fails.  So, while it was using its temporary
file, the file system did fill up.  Then when it exited, the temporary
file was removed and the space freed.

Solutions include:

1.  Moving /tmp to its own, larger file system (not a bad idea anyway).

2.  Taking advantage of the ability (hopefully) of ar(1) to use an
    alternate temporary directory specified by the environment variable
    TMPDIR.

