In article <MCGRANT.95May18193732@wheezer.stanford.edu>,
Michael C. Grant <mcgrant@wheezer.Stanford.EDU> wrote:
> In article <id.7YZJ1.ME6@nmti.com> peter@nmti.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
>    I imagine you could fly with a little bit of pixie dust and a bag of happy
>    thoughts, but that doesn't make it true. You can't run cgi-bin scripts 
>    without a server.

> Oh please. Just because your average browser can't do it now doesn't
> make it so. If the browser can bypass a server to read a file from a
> local disk,

The browser is not bypassing the server to read a file from a local disk.
It's following a URL that says "read the file from a file system". The "file:"
URL is occasionally *interpreted* as an "ftp:" URL, but the RFC doesn't
define it that way.

Now then, there is a mechanism to get the browser to execute scripts under
the direction of an HTML document. This is normally turned off by default,
because it's just asking to be trojan-horsed. If you define a mime type
for x-sh and a corresponding mailcap entry that runs a shell for it, then
Mosaic will happily download and run a shell command.

The security implications of this are obvious. As are the security
implications of client-side cgi. One hopes they're turned off by default.
