Subject: Re: shared libraries
Date: 1995/05/22
Message-ID: <3pqcvs$seo@lead.zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 103111475
distribution: world
references: <3pq4go$765@info.epfl.ch>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: metsky@zk3.dec.com (David Metsky)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In the first compile
	cc -o testtata -L. test.c -ltata
you specifically linked libc.a so you got the static case.

In the second compile
	cc -o testc -L. test.c -lc
you let the linker find the right version of libc.  It looks for the shared
version first in /usr/shlib and since it found it, that's what you got.

>Obviously libtata is the exact same thing as libc. Why is libc dynamically
>linked whereas libtata is statically linked ? What's the point of .so libraries
>then ?

libtata.a is the same as /usr/lib/libc.a, not the same as /usr/shlib/libc.so.
Your compile said to look in . first for libraries, and it found libtata.a
which is a link to /usr/lib/libc.a so that's what you got.  If you created
a libtata.so and made a symbolic link to /usr/shlib/libc.so, you would have
gotten the shared library.

By default, when a library is included on the link line by -lxxx, the linker
will look for the shared library first, and the static library second.  There
is a list of directories to scan for the library and /usr/shlib is the
first place it looks.  If you put a specific file on the link line, the 
linker will take that.
-- 
