We too had an advfs I/O error on a DEC 3000/600 under OSF/1 3.0, with a 9 GB
external Micropolis disk running under AdvFS (not LSM though).  We had
numerous system panics over a two week period, and lost a small number of
files (maybe 20 out of three or four thousand).  DEC looked at the crash-data
and uerf output and told us it was definitely a hardware problem with the
disk.  We returned it to the suppliers (not DEC) and they tested it non-stop
for 5 days and had no errors at all.  A different supplier told us that it
was possible to upgrade the firmware on the disk drive, and that had solved
some similar problems that they had previously experienced.  So we sent the
disk to them for the firmware upgrade (cost: 80 GB pounds) and have since had
no problems for five weeks.  The upgrade may have solved the problem - but
five weeks may not be long enough to be sure.

Note that the possibility of upgrading the firmware may not be well known -
our first supplier had never heard about it and so couldn't do it for us.
Apparently the upgrade corrects some timing incompatibilities between Alpha
and disk - that's all I know.  I heard also that if you have an IBM PC
compatible with a SCSI port, then you can get a piece of free software to do
the upgrade for yourself.  Sounded a bit beyond us though.

Tim Osborn     Climatic Research Unit, UEA, Norwich, UK



