In article <D8Gppo.Kyv@vptt.ch>,
haeni_r@vptt.ch (Haeni Reto) wrote:

> In article 3mk@encomix.com, dune@encomix.com (David Deza) writes:
>>I'm  going to install Windows NT  in a DEC Alpha 2000-500 running
>>OSF/1,  I have read the manual but  I think the guys of Microsoft
>>don't  speak  my  language (of course  they  speak  english and I
>>don't).  So  I will thank all  the information you could send me,
>>I'm  specially interested in how to set  up a disk, a whole disk,
>>to  receive  Windows  NT  and prepare  a  boot  menu  to use both
>>operating system (OSF/1 and Windows NT).
> 
> I wanted to do the same thing on my DEC Alpha 2000-300 that I could have
> choosed at boot time with which OS I want to work. 
> After talking to Digital Support Switzerland, I decided not to install the
> Win NT as second OS. DEC sayd that the NT scans the disks quite heavy and puts
> a kind of a 'stamp' on disks which FS can not be read by the NT. Like this, the 
> disks with the OSF/1 becomes corrupt.
> They said that the only way to have both OS on the same machine is, that you 
> unconnect the disks with the OS where you dont need at this time. For this,
> you have to have externel SCSI disks, if you dont want to open the cover
> of the Alpha every time.

What really happens is this:

At Windows NT v3.5 install time, it scans all of the attached disks for
"viruses". The disk "signatures" of Digital UNIX and OpenVMS look like
viruses, so Windows NT refuses to install. The workaround is to detach
the Digital UNIX and/or OpenVMS disks just for the install. After the
install is complete, you can reattach the disks and leave them attached,
since this "virus scan" only happens at install time.

When you run Disk Administrator from Windows NT, it will recognise that
the Digital UNIX and OpenVMS disks don't have the Windows NT disk
signature on them, and ask you if you want to write it. Just say no. If
you say yes, the Windows NT signature will corrupt the Digital UNIX and/or
OpenVMS disks so as to render them unreadable by Digital UNIX and/or
OpenVMS.

So, you only have to attach/detach the disks once (at Windows NT install
time), and Windows NT will happily coexist with Digital UNIX and OpenVMS
on the same machine as long as you don't let loose the destructive
power of Disk Administrator.

The "virus scanning" ability of the Windows NT installation will be fixed
in a future version, quite possibly v3.51.

