I don't want to get into a potentially huge debate about free vs.
proprietary software, but there are a few points I would like to
make.

While it is true that free software often costs a lot of time to
set up, there are aspects in which it costs less time. In bash or
gdb, scrolling back through the command buffer with the cursor keys
simply works. Out of the box. With other shells (sh, csh, ksh) it is
either not possible, or I have to spend time setting it up. This
is a small detail, but can cost a lot of time - is it really _that_
difficult? The same goes for filename completion.

After all, VMS users can take for granted that scrolling back through
the history list works, why shouldn't that apply to Digital Unix?

What a lot of packagers of Unix software (and the free software people
are no exception) do not realise is that default settings matter.
The default settings are going to be those that 95% of the users always
use. However configurable your product is, most people will not spend
time setting it up. If the UPS-programmers are listening: Yellow is not
a good color to highlight things in on most monitors.

Having tried out UPS (partly as a result of reading this thread), I must
say it is an impressive program. But, yet again I am disappointed to see
a free program with a totally wierd GUI. Yes, I'm sure the scrollbar is
really nifty when you get used to it (for those who haven't seen it, the
listing is scrolled by pressing the left button and moving it slightly
up or down. The speed at which the listing scrolls up or down is
proportional to the distance between the mouse cursor and the point where
you started holding down the button). However, a Motif or Athena 3D
scrollbar, with _working_ cursor and page-up/down keys would simply have been
less trouble for the user. The whole user interface is of the 'great-when-you
get-to-know-it' type, but I would have preferred to find 'File', 'Edit',
'View' and 'Options' buttons along the top.

--
