newsgroups: comp.lang.fortran,comp.os.vms,comp.unix.osf.osf1,vmsnet.alpha


In article <3p7q83$mqs@sgi.iunet.it>, Glynn Field <field@saclantc.nato.int> 
writes:
|>I was pleased to see the recent announcement from Digital 
|>about Fortran 90 for Open VMS systems. I was also pleased 
|>to see that it will be fully integrated with the LSE and 
|>the debugger. 
|>
|>Given that Fortran 90 will compile existing FORTRAN77 
|>programs what is Digital�s policy on future support
|>for FORTRAN77? 
|>
|>Is it the intention that FORTRAN77 will become unsupported
|>and Fortran 90 be the Digital Fortran compiler?

At this time, we are selling and supporting our F90 and F77 compilers as
separate products.  DEC Fortran 90 is not yet capable of being a complete
replacement for DEC Fortran (F77) as it does not have all of the latter's
features.  This is especially true on OpenVMS where the Fortran 90 compiler
lacks support for the DICTIONARY statement (Common Data Dictionary definitions),
the DEC Source Code Analyzer component of the DECset tools, and minor features
such as /NAMES=AS_IS and CDEC$ ALIAS (the latter two are also differences
on Digital UNIX).

It is our plan to, at some time in the future, have DEC Fortran 90 take over
from DEC Fortran.  We have a lot of work to do before that is possible, and
what form a merging of products would take is yet to be determined.  Until
that time, we are continuing active development and maintenance of DECFortran.

If you are a current DEC Fortran user, and are not looking to use Fortran 90
in the near future, then by all means stick with DEC Fortran - you'll get
the features, performance, reliability and support you've come to expect from
Digital.  We've added some Fortran 90 control constructs, such as labeled
blocks, CASE, CYCLE and EXIT so you can start taking advantage of those
right away.  If you have an immediate need for Fortran 90, or if you'd like
to use all of Fortran 90 and don't use the few DEC Fortran features not
yet included in DEC Fortran 90, then buying or switching to DEC Fortran 90
is an open option.

DEC Fortran has some 30 years of heritage behind it.  While we've been very
pleased with the tremendous success of DEC Fortran 90 in the year it's been
available on Digital UNIX, we want to make sure that current DEC Fortran
users encounter as few "surprises" as possible - keep in mind that the
Fortran 90 language definition requires some changes in semantics which might
affect some DEC Fortran users (the addition of PAD='YES' as the default in
OPEN is one which comes to mind.)

The release notes for DEC Fortran 90 V2.0 (for both Digital UNIX and
OpenVMS Alpha) are available for perusal at:

   ftp.digital.com /pub/Digital/info/document/fortran90-alpha-unix-rnotes.txt
                                              fortran90-alpha-vms-rnotes.txt

If you'd like to try DEC Fortran 90 for free, telnet to either
axposf.pa.dec.com (Digital UNIX) or axpvms.pa.dec.com (OpenVMS) and log in
as axpguest, no password.  You'll be given a form to fill in to apply for
a free account.  Try compiling and running your application there to see how
it works with DEC Fortran 90, or just play with the language.

If you have particular concerns or questions - please feel free to write us
at fortran@digital.com.
