In article <1995May16.140059.43418@cobra.uni.edu>,
	Christian Kuhtz <kuhtzc0580@cobra.uni.edu> wrote:

>>>Personally, I'd like to see [sysadmin] tools migrate to HTML 3, so that
>>>conventional Web browsers can be used to operate them -- Mosaic
>>>(for instance) in GUI environments and Lynx in character-based
>>>systems, sharing a common look-and-feel throughout. Is anyone up
>>>to it?

>Oh yes, please!, and open more security holes a la SATAN :)... I'd really like
>to invite the entire web for a cooperative system administration session....

HTML is a text formatting standard, not a security system. Doing system
administration using HTML scripts (as opposed to FMLI, for example) has
nothing to do with whatever engine is used to format these scripts or
what security is implied.

I see no reason why the CGI scripts used to perform the tasks requested
through HTML forms couldn't be secured using conventional Unix security
techniques.

>Why use HTML in the first place and not plain X11?!

By definition, X11 programs won't work on character-based systems, or
non-X GUI systems. The whole point of my original post was to have a
system that would be immediately portable to many display environments.

Besides, "plain X11" can mean a lot of things. Since you want a system
that doesn't take forever to develop and can easily be enhanced locally,
I don't think it would be a good idea to do it in "C". If HTML is
rejected, I could live with admin scripts written in wksh, but that's in
limbo because CDE's scripting enviroment might be different. And it's
still much harder to do than HTML.

No, I don't think there's anything wrong with HTML in this context at
all. It's quite suitable. Since UnixWare systems ship with perl, they're
halfway there already :-).

