In a previous article, peter@nmti.com (Peter da Silva) says:

>In article <D8L1Jw.A1G@telly.telly.org>,
>Evan Leibovitch <evan@telly.telly.org> wrote:
>> Personally, I'd like to see these tools migrate to HTML 3, so that
>> conventional Web browsers can be used to operate them -- Mosaic
>> (for instance) in GUI environments and Lynx in character-based
>> systems, sharing a common look-and-feel throughout. Is anyone up
>> to it?
>
>I have started writing cgi scripts to run system information tools via
>Mosaic. The big problem with going all the way to system administration
>with this is the security issue. How do you keep just anyone from attaching
>to your server and issuing administration commands?

The simplest answer is to ask for a password.  This can be done either by
having a password field on the form or by putting your scripts in a
directory with a .htaccess file.  You'll have to be careful about people
packet-sniffing your passwords, though.  Using one-time passwords might not
be a bad idea.

You can also restrict users by IP address, but that's subject to IP
spoofing.

For my group management page (http://k2.cwru.edu/groups.html) I used IP
address checking and a password on the form, although security wasn't a big
issue do to the program's limited functionality.

The best way to be secure is probably to use the Netscape Commerce Server,
but unless you already have one that's a costly solution. :)
