In article <1995May10.152228.21676@newsserver.rrzn.uni-hannover.de>, nhglmeng@rrzn-user.uni-hannover.de () writes:

>Hi,
>we've a DEC Alpha 3000/600 with OSF1 (oh - Digital Unix ??).
>In a few days we'll get a new HDD (the third, up to now there
>are a 1 GB and 2 GB). I've to submit that we're not too experienced
>with OSF (very new workstation), thus we would really appreciate
>any suggestions concerning the installation of the harddisk (e.g.
>the most important steps like kernel handling and so on, hints for
>'dangerous' steps during the installation, possibly occuring traps 
>..........).

1.  Read the hardware guides for information about installation
    of the new disk.  Select unused to SCSI ID and set the drive
    to that ID and with the system shutdown add it to the config.
    Verify that the new disk is seen by the console using the
    appropriate console command (often "show device").

2.  When the system boots be on the look out for the rz# name
    associated with the drive.  For disks on bus 0, it will
    just be the SCSI target ID, but after that will be:

	(bus * 8) + target-id

    If the device isn't noticed at boot, it may just be because you
    have a version of OSF that wants a line in the configuration for
    it.  If it matters you can fix this later.

3.  With the system booted created the special device files for
    the new disk.

	# cd /dev
	# ./MAKEDEV rz#

    To verify that OSF sees the disk:

	# file /dev/rrz#c

    (fill in # with the device number)

4.  Recent versions of Digital UNIX will be very happy to use the
    drive without any changes to the configuration file, but
    there may be dependencies on an entry in the configuration
    file depending on the version.  The simplest way to update
    the configuration is to boot genvmunix as vmunix and rebuild
    the configuration file.  This is documented in one system
    management guides.

    If you're confortable making small changes to the configuration
    file, then borrowing appropriate lines from the GENERIC configuration
    file can save some time.

5.  Label the disk.  If this is a 3rd party disk, don't worry that
    there may not be disktab entry for it.  Digital UNIX will query
    the driver for the disks default partition table and geometry.
    If you want to partition the drive, do so here.

6.  Create file systems with newfs(8), add it an LSM disk group, create
    an Advfs domain or add it to an existing Advfs domain.  It really
    depends on your currently managing space.

I think newfs(8) is smart enough to notice when a file system is
already mounted on a device, so it won't let you create a new
file system on an already mounted partition.  Still about the
only dangerous thing you have to watch out for is using the
wrong device name when write to the disk.

>Thanks in advance for any hints,
>Falko
>
