In article <3oqvsh$dpm@lll-winken.llnl.gov>, oberman@icaen.llnl.gov writes:
> This is actually a BSD Reno netetwork code problem and is not specific to
> Digital UNIX. I found a note from Stevens describing the problem in some
> detail, but basically it resolves to:
> You set the IP options to specify a loose source route. You then set the TTL
> to 1. Unfortunately the code to do raw socet options wipe out any IP options,
> so it does not work. You cna force the TTL or you can set IP options, but not
> both.

Well, I was hoping I was wrong but it looks like the network code is
determined to stop you from doing this.

I found the note from Stevens.  I tried to implement the workaround
he suggested but never had time to do, and create my own ip options
along with the IP and UDP headers.  Somewhere deep in the network kernel
it resets the IP header length byte but leaves the option data.  So
you end up with weird UDP port numbers in the packets.

Most annoying.  Well, at least TGV Multinet on VMS does it right.
