>We have an AXP 3000/600 running OSF1 v2.0 to which we have attached a
>Seagate ST410800N 9GB disk.  This drive has been working reasonably well,
...
>Apr 19 15:57:33 nssdc vmunix:  Medium Error bad block number: 1458235
>Apr 19 15:57:33 nssdc vmunix: Hard Error Detected
...
>It looks a little like a bad block.  I've read a bunch of man pages and
>I'm at a loss to know what to do with a bad block.  I thought modern
>SCSI drives dealt with them internally.  I can't seem to find a disk
>maintenance program for rz disks.  I'm I missing something?  I have
>since found other files that I am unable to read, so the error isn't
>confined to this one file.
...
>--  James W. Williams               NASA Goddard Space Flight Center 
>--  williams@nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov    Code 633
>--  +1 301 286-1131                 Greenbelt, MD 20771


James, try the Digital UNIX / DEC OSF/1 SCSI Utility "scu":
Unmount the disk before you start.

# scu -f /dev/rrz14c		# "c" partition = whole drive
scu> show device
scu> show capacity
scu> show defects
scu> verify media		# tries to read all blocks
				# write down the defect blocks
				# if not more than 20 bad blocks:
scu> reassign lba  <nnnnn>

If this does not help, I would return the drive to the dealer.
Most vendors give 5 years warranty on large drives.
But you can also try:

scu> format defects all

which destroys all data on the drive.

I hope this helps.
-- 
Siggy Hensel    < siggy@aed-graphics.de >



