In article <bhunt.799968543@keck2.umd.edu>, bhunt@keck2.umd.edu (Brian R. Hunt) writes:
>...it never did handle negative X and Y offsets correctly

This is a documented restriction of dxterm.  It only accepts NW gravity
geometry parameters (i.e., +x+y only).

>OSF/1 3.0 the problem is much worse--I can't even get a placement like
>+100+100 to work (unless I use no window manager, or an old version of
>mwm).

Hmm, it works fine for me.  However, you need to make sure that the
default resource file (~/DXterm or whatever you specified with -setup)
does not contain the lines DXterm.x and DXterm.y.

I'd rather not go into the reasons why dxterm behaves this way.  Let's
just say that it carries a lot of excess baggage that gets in the way.

Alfred

