Hang on. I think the original problem with `ls -l' not working is because the
wrong `ls' image was copied to /usr/ftp/bin. I came across this when setting up
an anonymous FTP area. I followed the instructions which said copy /bin/ls to
/usr/ftp/bin/ls. Using this image, `ls -l' didn't work. Later I spotted the much
larger image /sbin/ls. Copying /sbin/ls to /usr/ftp/bin/ls gave me a working
'ls -l'.

I'd be wary of copying SIA stuff to an anonymous FTP area.

