|>What's about UPS that is not in DECladebug? Easy: it's got a GUI. Does
|>DECladebug have it? No. You need it separately by getting Fuse.
|>	Ease of use.

We've known this was a problem.  Our solution is that the GUI will be 
packaged with DECladebug starting with our next release.  You won't need 
to buy FUSE just to get the DECladebug GUI.  

|>What's about UPS that's not in DECladebug? Easy, it's free. Does DECladebug
|>come free? No, you need the Developer's license. OK, if you are on a
|>DECcampus schedule, there's no problem. If you aren't?
|>	Cost.

You can get the appropriate license for as little as $350 (single-user, 
no C compiler).  Even the unlimited license with a C compiler is about
$1500.  That's the standard pricing; if DECcampus is cheaper, then that's
even better.  

Furthermore, while the source for UPS is free, it takes your time to 
build it.  Your time is worth something - and you'll have to make the
tradeoff about how long it takes to become more expensive to build a 
"free" product.

|>
|>What's about UPS that's not in DECladebug? Easy, the GUI is also free.
|>FUSE does not come with the Development license. It has to be bought
|>separately.
|>	More cost.

Which will go away soon.

By the way, you can use DECladebug from within emacs, using the standard
emacs interface.  That's a cheap, standard UI to use.  The support has
been available for some time; check the release notes for more details.

|>
|>What's about UPS that's not in DECladebug? Easy, the source code is
|>available. Is it for DECladebug? I'd bet that if it is it is only if
|>you pay big bucks.
|>	Much more costs.

The sources aren't available, as far as I'm aware - but why do you 
want them?  If it's because you're going to make source changes, then
your cost argument goes right out the window.  If you're going to 
make source changes, you're going to have to understand and support
those changes.  That takes your time, and that costs your employer.
As these changes are distributed around the net, you've got to fold
the new changes in to your version.  That's more time and more money.

With DECladebug, you have a good, dedicated support staff working full 
time to make improvements and fix bugs.  We know this code inside out.

|>
|>What's about UPS that's not in DECladebug? Easy, it is portable to multiple
|>platforms. You learn it once and use the same in different architectures.
|>In many of them. How much architectures provide DECladebug? And if there
|>are several, are all supported with the same license or do you need
|>rather separate licenses for each architecture? Does DECladebug support
|>GCC and other compilers?
|>	Portability and consistency.

DECladebug supports several platforms.  It is very portable.  The GUI
actually supports even more platforms, since the code is shared with the
OpenVMS Debugger on both Alpha and VAX.  DECladebug is available on all
of the Digital platforms that it makes Digital business sense for it to 
be on.  If there was a business case for porting DECladebug to non-Digital
platforms, Digital would consider it.  (It isn't hard, given our design.  
We know because we've ported or investigated porting DECladebug to some
interesting places.)

Besides, you say "dbx, gdb, ups, will offer a common interface."  Since
DECladebug accepts the dbx command set, then it falls into the same 
category.  You learn the dbx command set and you can go anywhere, 
including DECladebug.  (To be honest, we're *almost* to 100% acceptance 
of the dbx command set.  We're 95% of the way there in the currently 
available release; the remainder is in the works.)

The different architectures do need different licenses.  Just because you
have MIPS Ultrix doesn't mean you get Digital UNIX for Alpha for free.

While we do not support GCC, DECladebug does work with it.  (As one of 
the DECladebug symbol table folks, gcc support has been on my list of 
things to look into.)

On the other hand, gdb, UPS, and the other debuggers don't have the 
advantage of working directly with the Digital compiler and operating 
systems people.  Thus, DECladebug had good, solid thread support shipped
with DEC OSF/1 3.0 - when the thread support became available.  People
wanting Fortran-90 debugging support got it with their compiler.  Much
of the work going into our next release is stuff the competition won't
have for quite some time.

|>
|>Of course, I see advantages in DECladebug. It's got some nice features,
|>and is supported and backed by a strong company (I mean DEC :-) obviously)
|>and if you have all the licenses, packages, and enough hardware, it is
|>a good program. I use it indeed from time to time. And I don't mean to
|>say decladebug has none of the above qualities, only that the extent
|>is different.
|>
|>My only complain about commercial tools is that they are often not
|>effectively cost-effective. Many times one is requested outrageous
|>prices. And even whent that's not the case, directly, there use to have
|>enormous indirect costs. I can understand many people worrying...

I don't see $350-$1500 as an outrageous price to pay for a good 
commercial debugger.  Given that there are a lot of indirect costs with 
all your "free" software, it looks downright cheap.  Do the math at
your place - add up all the time you spend supporting UPS.  You may
be surprised.  (Try doing the experiment for emacs some time.  The
numbers may be frightening!)

|>
|>For example: One could think that with DECcampus the cost/benefit should
|>be very good. *But* if you work in an heterogeneous environment, it fades
|>away very fast: dbx, gdb, ups, will offer a common interface. With
|>non-standard tools you need to master different command sets and interfaces
|>for each architecture and OS. You begin to lose time. Time is money and
|>at some point you are losing more than winning.
|>
|>I think that DECladebug is a nice, non-standard, non-GUI, not user-portable
|>tool.
|>

I'd say that DECladebug uses standard commands. (dbx command set, EMACS 
interface) DECladebug will have a very nice, customizable GUI ship with 
it for no extra cost in the next release.  It's not user-portable.  It's
cheap.

Having gotten this far, I find it interesting that there's nothing about 
C language support that got on to your list - which is what prompted my
original question.  Is there something special about the UPS C support
that you particularly like?  Are there things you dislike?

I think there are a lot of reasons for choosing DECladebug.  I want to
give you even more - which is why I appreciate your answers.
