>>>>> "Aris" == Aris Theocharides <aris@mundoe.maths.mu.OZ.AU> writes:
In article <9512219.10409@mulga.cs.mu.OZ.AU> aris@mundoe.maths.mu.OZ.AU (Aris Theocharides) writes:


Aris> I am attempting to add a disk to an OSF/1 3.0 system. I have 2
Aris> internal drives OK, but want to add a third (external) drive.

It depends on what type of system you are using.  The 3000/600 has two
SCSI busses, so what you are trying to do is the right idea, just a
little mixed up in details.

If the system only has one SCSI bus (check with: "show device" at the
console prompt) then you just need to add more entries in the
configuration file.

Aris> Can anyone let me know what the format structure of the conf
Aris> file for disks on the second scsi adapter are? I am only
Aris> guessing with the rz1 entry below.

Assuming you are using a 3000/600, this is what you want:

bus             tcds0      at tc0       slot  6 vector    tcdsintr 
controller      scsi0      at tcds0     slot  0 
controller      scsi1      at tcds0     slot  1 

device disk     rz0        at scsi0     drive     0 
device disk     rz1        at scsi0     drive     8 
device disk     rz2        at scsi0     drive     16 
device disk     rz3        at scsi0     drive     24
device disk     rz4        at scsi0     drive     32 
device disk     rz5        at scsi0     drive     40 
device disk     rz6        at scsi0     drive     48
device disk     rz7        at scsi0     drive     56
device disk     rz8        at scsi1     drive     64
device disk     rz9        at scsi1     drive     72
device disk     rz10       at scsi1     drive     80
device disk     rz11       at scsi1     drive     88
device disk     rz12       at scsi1     drive     96
device disk     rz13       at scsi1     drive     104
device disk     rz14       at scsi1     drive     112
device disk     rz15       at scsi1     drive     120
device disk     rz16       at scsi1     drive     128


Notice how the last numbers increase continuously.  You had tried to
reset the numbers back down to 8 for your other drive.  Also, hooking
up the drive to the system and booting genvmunix will allow you to
configure you system properly with the new drive.

I personally like to edit the conf file right from the start to
include all possible drives in the kernel, so I don't have to keep
changing it if I move drives around.

John

