X-Deja-AN: 101876998
references: <3nrief$366@tenebris.rutgers.edu: <saunders-2904951815390001@caspar.che.wisc.edu: <3o34dd$41o@tenebris.rutgers.edu>
organization: The Ohio State University, Department of Computer and Information Science
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

You may want to look at the settings of some kernal parameters. Ubcmaxpercent
and ubcborrowpercent are a couple of good ones - they control the size of
the unified buffer cache. There's also a parameter called ubcminpercent, I
think. I believe that you'll also find one called bufcache, which controls
the initial size of the buffer cache.  Since you have 1GB of ram, the defaults
for these things are setting your buffer cache at 100MB at system startup,
and it grows from there.  It can consume all of memory on a disk intensive
system, and result in paging.

The assumption that you need 2X memory for swap is an interesting piece of
lore - what you really need is enough swap to avoid running out of VM. You
will use less real memory if you have the system set to "lazy" evaluation
(remove /sbin/swapdefault).  If carefully tuned, the system need NEVER
swap if you have enough ram.  If you do swap, and you run out of swap space
in "lazy" mode, things can get ugly.  For what it's worth, I worked for some
months on a multiprocessor 7000 series machine with 8GB of memory and only
a couple of hundred megs of swap space - once tuned, the machine NEVER swapped
with the application load we had.  The performance was awesome.

By the way, check out the sysconfig and sysconfigdb commands - you don't have
to rebuild the kernel to set this stuff. That's a really nice feature of
Digital UNIX.  Oh, before I forget, the AdvFS maintains its own, separate
buffer cache.  Check the settings on that, too, if you are using AdvFS.

Hope that helps.

Eric Osborne




