In <3o6b5t$bmg@agate.berkeley.edu> peterm@tonks.EECS.Berkeley.EDU (Peter Mardahl) writes:
>DEC UNIX's (aka osf1) kernel is very huge and consumes
>lots of precious RAM--8M if you use an out-of-the-box
>kernel and 5M as I've configured the kernel.
>I've considered stripping the /vmunix binary to reduce
>its usage of RAM.

You can't reduce RAM usage by stripping (in the sense of /usr/bin/strip)
the kernel. You could free some RAM by removing unneeded device drivers
and optional features, although the installations procedures should have
done it for you (doconfig and friends).

You can free some disk space by not including all non global
symbols into the /vmunix symbol table: I got the /vmunix down
to around 3 MB disk space by using
makeoptions     LDOPTS="-x"
in the kernel config file. (This is for a DEC 3000 Model 300)
But beware: this might be unsupported by Digital and it makes some
kernel debugging tools useless.

>Screwing up the kernel is a big nightmare so I'm not
>going to experiment without asking first:

Either have /genvmunix around on the root file system or a bootable
CD and a CD ROM player.

>so here goes:
>Will a stripped kernel work?
>What besides ps, uptime will break?

Stripping in the sense of /usr/bin/strip: no, it won't work,
too much of /sbin/init.d/* depends on 'ps' to work.

>Is my theory that a smaller /vmunix on disk will cause
>the RAM used to be smaller correct?

No, the symbol table will not be loaded into memory and will
not consume RAM.

