armhold> We have a Sable 2100 with 1 GB of real memory.  It's
armhold> configured with 196MB swap on it's primary disk, and 1GB swap
armhold> on a second disk.  I keep getting syslog (I assume it's
armhold> syslog) warnings: "swap space below 10 percent free", when
armhold> there is really plenty of swap space available, as shown by
armhold> vmstat and top.

What does 'swapon -s' say when this happens?  I don't really trust
vmstat or top to report swap utilization.

By the way, your 1 GB of swap is not going to get much use.  Your
system isn't going to page until physical memory is used up, but since
you only have as much swap as physical memory (and are using eager
allocation mode), you will run out of swap as soon as physical memory
fills up!  If this system is being used for a small number of very
memory intensive jobs, I would switch it to lazy swap allocation.

By the way (2), your article arrived here with the References: line
