>wonder if there's something they aren't telling us. :^)

One of the most common questions asked about DEC OSF/1 (the older
versions), is what the 10% warning means.  There are so many people
that know the stock answer, that we don't need to post about it.
It may not be so much "avoid", but that its been answered by someone
else by the time one of us sees it.

If I'm reading news from home, I may answer the question directly
to the person asking because I don't have all the rn pieces needed
to post follow-ups, but mail I can do.

Swap space usage problem seemed to be far more common on older
versions of DEC OSF/1 (V1.2, V1.3).  V1.3 is nearly three years
old now and been replaced by V2.0, V2.1, V3.0 and now V3.2.  Since
some of the swap space short problem were particular to how V1.2
and V1.3 allocated space, these problems are long gone in the
current versions.

Maybe the 10% warning message needs a good explaination note
in the FAQ, if doesn't already have one.
