nmovold@ibl.bm (Neil Movold) writes:

>Has anyone successfully been able to use /bin/false as a shell
>in the /etc/passwd file in order to not allow users to telnet into
>restricted FTP accounts?  I want to be able to allow users to ftp
>into my server to another root directory other than the anonymous
>ftp directory in order to do WWW development.  However, I don't
>want them to be able to telnet into the server.  There is some great
>documentation on setting up this sort of environment in the book
>"Managing INTERNET Information Services" on page 80 under
>Group Controls.  The procedure is straight forward but does not
>work.  The book says to put the shell /bin/false on the end of the
>users /etc/passwd file entry which would block telnet, but this
>does not work since if a user then FTPs into the FTP server,
>they get an access denied error message.

>Any suggestions would be appreciated.

Add '/bin/false' to the file /etc/shells. ftp won't let you in
unless you have a valid shell, and /etc/shells is what defines this.

