dsr@lns598.lns.cornell.edu (Daniel S. Riley) writes:
dsr> By the way, your 1 GB of swap is not going to get much use.  Your
dsr> system isn't going to page until physical memory is used up,

In article <3oarvj$696@tenebris.rutgers.edu>
armhold@tenebris.rutgers.edu (George Armhold) writes:
armhold> Huh?  It's set to eager swap mode- and therefore allocates a
armhold> page of swap for every page of real memory allocated.

Yes, it allocates a page of backing store for every page of real
memory--my point is that it hardly ever uses it.  It won't actually
page until it runs out of memory, and there's only a narrow region
where it is out of memory and still has swap available.  You have a GB
of disk that is being used by the VM system for bookkeeping purposes
but is almost never written to or read.

dsr> but since you only have as much swap as physical memory (and are
dsr> using eager allocation mode), you will run out of swap as soon as
dsr> physical memory fills up!  

armhold> No, I said I have two swap partitions, the first (primary) is
armhold> 196MB and the second is 1GB.  So the total swap is 1220MB.

So you have a narrow region between 1GB and 1.2GB where the system
does page, doing some reads and writes to the swap partition.  I
suppose the system will page somewhat before the 1 GB point due to the
file system buffer cache, but I am still unconvinced that your swap
configuration makes sense.  (Of course, none of this directly
addresses your real problem with the apparently bogus low swap space
warnings.)

