armhold@tenebris.rutgers.edu (George Armhold) wrote:
>
 
> Eager swap mode, with swapdefault pointing to the 196MB swap
> partition. The general "rule of thumb" in swap space allocation is 2 X
> phys mem, however given that we have 1GB of real memory we don't
> really expect to need a 2GB address space (then again, ...)  I could
> increase the swap space, however I don't think this will solve the
> problem since we're seeing the messages well before we're at the 10
> percent level.  My guess is that whatever generates the warnings isn't
> considering the fact that swap is spread out over two devices.
> 
> Oh, and I forget to mention in my original posting that this was with
> OSF/1 3.2.
> 
> -George


Something doesn't seem to be set up correctly.  The system thinks
it's nearly out of swap, whereas you think you have plenty.  Have
you checked to be sure that the system sees your secondary swap
disk?  Check the output of this command:

/sbin/swapon -s

It should list each swap partition separately, and then the
total available.  If it doesn't list your second swap partition,
then it isn't being used -- check /etc/fstab to see if the second
swap partition is correctly listed.   If it is, check and see if
the disk is online. 

You can also add the second swap partition on the fly, as follows
(assume that you're adding /dev/rz3c as a swap partition):

/sbin/swapon /dev/rz3c


			George Chaltas



