Hi,

  I have a 3000/600, 196M memory, enough swap space. The problem: I
have one process which needs about 120M of (virtual) memory. I
did nice it to 20, which according to the man pagve should mean
that it runs ONLY when nothing else wants to run. Apparently this 
is not happening. Whenever this process runs, no other process
can run at 'full speed'. 
  My feeling: since all other processes are heavily I/O bound,
they get into an I/O wait state (especially when the big process
is getting to swap out), the system sees no CPU requests, so starts
to load the big process back, which puts even more load on the
I/O subsystem, etc. The process does not actually run, but is being swaped
in/out constantly and ties up the I/O subsystem...
  What can I do? The only solution that I could come up is sending
a STOP (and later on a CONTINUE) signal to the memory pig process.
Sure I can invent some daemon the does this automatically, but I
don't want to reinvent the wheel.
  Ideas?

Thanks,
  Gyula

