reply-to: davis@space.mit.edu
newsgroups: comp.editors,comp.os.ultrix,comp.unix.osf.osf1,alt.lang.s-lang

 
 >We're moving a large numbers of users from a mainframe environment to
 >Unix. Some of them have large data files which can be described in the
 >following manner: (trivial example)
 >	Columns  Description
 >	-------  -----------
 >	1-4	 id number
 >	5-24     name
 >	25-30    dat1
 >	30-35    dat2
 >	...	 etc
 >In essence, they've got what I call fixed column data. (not whitespace
 >separated - I'm mainframe-challenged, sorry for the wording) They're
 >used to using Stanford Wylbur (not the same as some commercial Wylbur
 >implementations) to do their editing tasks.
 
 >They do things like:
 
 >  Find the lines that have '1234' in columns 1-4 and change
 >  columns 30-35 to 'abcdef'.

There are many ways to do this using JED.  For example, one could use
regular expression searches, keyboard macros, etc...  However, if this is a
routine task, I suggest something like the following function:

   define solution_1 ()
   {
     push_spot ();   % save position
     bob ();         % goto beginning of the buffer
     while (bol_fsearch ("1234"))
       {
          goto_column (30);
	  deln (6);
	  insert ("abcdef");
       }
     pop_spot ();    % goto original position 
   }
     
 
 >  Find the lines with '1234' in columns 1-4 and 'dog' starting in
 >  column 5 and insert three spaces starting in column 25.

   define solution_2 ()
   {
     push_spot ();   % save position
     bob ();         % goto beginning of the buffer
     while (bol_fsearch ("1234dog"))
       {
          goto_column (25);
	  insert ("   ");
       }
     pop_spot ();    % goto original position 
   }

 >  Find the lines with 'dog' starting in column 5 and copy columns 25-30
 >  to 30-35.
 
   define solution_3 ()
   {
     push_spot ();   % save position
     bob ();         % goto beginning of the buffer
     while (re_fsearch ("^....dog"))
       {
          goto_column (25);
	  push_mark ();
	  go_right (5);          % Now at column 30 so insert marked region
	  insert (bufsubstr ());
       }
     pop_spot ();    % goto original position 
   }
 
 
 >  Find the lines longer than 35 characters and replace columns 5-24
 >  with 'invalid' (left justified, blank padded)
 
   define solution_4 ()
   {
     push_spot ();   % save position
     bob ();         % goto beginning of the buffer
     do 
       {
          eol ();            % goto end of line
	  if (what_column () > 35)
	    {
	       goto_column (5);
	       deln (19);
	       insert ("invalid");
	       insert_spaces (12);
	    }
       }
     while (down (1));
     pop_spot ();    % goto original position 
   }

The latest version of jed (0.97-6) is available from space.mit.edu in
pub/davis/jed.

--John





